WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.199
A SELECT against a ten-billion-tuple Listens table does not run on one machine.

00:00:05.199 --> 00:00:08.398
It runs in a building the size of a Costco.

00:00:08.398 --> 00:00:11.598
Thousands of servers.

00:00:11.598 --> 00:00:14.797
The data spread across all of them.

00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:17.996
The cost of each reed depends on which tier the data sits in.

00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:21.195
Latency in a modern data center spans seven orders of magnitude.

00:00:21.195 --> 00:00:24.395
One nanosecond in L one cache.

00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:27.594
A hundred milliseconds across continents.

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:30.793
That gap is what every algorithm in this course is fighting.

00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:34.007
Look at the tiers.

00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:37.220
L one cache, one nanosecond, on the chip.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.434
RAM, a hundred nanoseconds, on the board.

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:43.647
SSD, a hundred microseconds, same rack.

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:46.861
Spinning disk, ten milliseconds.

00:00:46.861 --> 00:00:50.075
Network inside the data center, half a millisecond.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:53.288
Cross-region, fifty milliseconds.

00:00:53.288 --> 00:00:56.502
Cross-continent, a hundred milliseconds.

00:00:56.502 --> 00:00:59.715
Each tier is a hundred to a thousand times slower than the one above.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:02.929
Touch the wrong tier and you pay ten million times the price.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.643
Google's data centers.

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:08.357
Hundreds of thousands of square feet.

00:01:08.357 --> 00:01:11.071
Millions of servers.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.785
Custom switches.

00:01:13.785 --> 00:01:16.500
Custom chips.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.214
These are the nerve centers for BigQuery, Cloud SQL, and Spanner.

00:01:19.214 --> 00:01:21.928
Every server is a node in a giant distributed query system.

00:01:21.928 --> 00:01:24.642
Run a search and dozens of these machines work in parallel.

00:01:24.642 --> 00:01:27.356
Each one fetching its slice of the index from the right tier.

00:01:27.356 --> 00:01:30.255
Azure tells the same story from the Microsoft side.

00:01:30.255 --> 00:01:33.154
Buildings the size of city blocks.

00:01:33.154 --> 00:01:36.053
Cooling systems that draw water from nearby rivers.

00:01:36.053 --> 00:01:38.953
Inside, the same storage hierarchy, at a much larger scale.

00:01:38.953 --> 00:01:41.852
Tens of millions of cores.

00:01:41.852 --> 00:01:44.751
Exabytes of storage.

00:01:44.751 --> 00:01:47.650
A global network connecting it all.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.362
Why does a database course care about concrete and copper?

00:01:50.362 --> 00:01:53.074
Because a query plan is a routing decision.

00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:55.786
Which tier do you reed from.

00:01:55.786 --> 00:01:58.498
How often.

00:01:58.498 --> 00:02:01.211
In what order.

00:02:01.211 --> 00:02:03.923
Get the tier right and a billion-tuple query finishes in seconds.

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.635
Get it wrong and the same query never finishes.

00:02:06.635 --> 00:02:09.347
The cost model we use next is a clean abstraction over this hierarchy.

00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:12.059
Count the slow tier.

00:02:12.059 --> 00:02:14.771
Ignore the fast tiers.

00:02:14.771 --> 00:02:18.037
The bigger the data, the more the cost model bends.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.304
Modern databases are built around these buildings.

00:02:21.304 --> 00:02:24.570
You cannot understand a query plan without understanding where the data lives.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.836
Next we collapse this whole hierarchy into one unit.

00:02:27.836 --> 00:02:31.103
A page.

00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:34.369
And we count pages instead of milliseconds.
